<Article slug="avoca-alternatives-under-3m">
  <p>
    Avoca announced in April 2026 that it had raised more than $125 million across its Seed, Series A and Series B
    rounds at a billion-dollar valuation, and says it is on track to book a billion dollars of jobs through its platform
    this year. If you run a home services business and have been looking at AI, you have seen
    their name. This is an honest look at who they are built for, and what to consider if that is not you.
  </p>

  <h2>What Avoca is</h2>
  <p>
    It is not a phone answering app. Avoca's system answers inbound calls, gathers job details, books appointments
    directly into the CRM, updates customer records, qualifies leads in real time, routes complex situations to humans,
    and follows up on pending estimates automatically. It understands service categories properly: an HVAC tune-up is
    not an emergency plumbing call, and it captures different information for each.
  </p>
  <p>
    The anchor is the ServiceTitan integration. That is a deliberate and smart choice, because ServiceTitan is used by a
    large majority of professional home services companies doing more than $3 million in annual revenue. Build for where
    the money is.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is genuinely right for</h2>
  <p>
    If you run ServiceTitan, do more than $3 million a year, and have at least one person whose job includes customer
    service operations, Avoca is probably the best product you can buy. We say that on our own comparison page. Do not
    let a smaller company talk you out of the better-resourced option when the better-resourced option fits.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the fit breaks down</h2>
  <p>Three situations where shops find themselves outside the target:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>You are on Housecall Pro or Jobber, not ServiceTitan.</b> Most one-to-three truck shops are. The integration
      depth that makes a platform valuable is specific to the CRM it was built around.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>You are well under $3 million.</b> Enterprise-shaped products carry enterprise-shaped minimums and onboarding
      processes. Below a certain size the economics stop working in your favour.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Nobody owns systems at your shop.</b> Platforms assume a person who configures, monitors and improves them. If
      that person is you, between jobs, at 8pm, it will not get done.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The actual alternatives, honestly</h2>

  <h3>1. An AI answering app ($45 to $299 a month)</h3>
  <p>
    Voksha, Call Agent AI, Super and similar. They answer the phone well and set up in an afternoon. Right choice if
    your only problem is unanswered calls and you have someone to maintain the configuration. Wrong choice if the value
    is in connecting the phone to your CRM, your estimates and your invoices.
  </p>

  <h3>2. A traditional answering service</h3>
  <p>
    Real humans, message-taking, typically per-call pricing. Better than voicemail, and some customers still prefer a
    person. The weakness is the same as it has always been: they do not know your calendar, your service area or your
    customers, so you are still doing the real work in the morning.
  </p>

  <h3>3. A done-for-you managed service</h3>
  <p>
    Someone builds the workflow around your existing software, runs it, and keeps tuning it. More expensive than an app,
    cheaper than an enterprise platform's realistic all-in cost, and the maintenance is somebody's job rather than
    yours. This is the category we are in, so weigh the recommendation accordingly.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Fix the scheduling first, not the phone</h3>
  <p>
    Worth saying because nobody selling software says it: if your real problem is that you are booked three weeks out
    and turning work away, answering more calls faster will not help. Sort capacity first.
  </p>

  <h2>Five questions for any vendor in this space</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Which CRM do you integrate with most deeply, and what happens with mine specifically?</li>
    <li>What does it do when it recognises an emergency, and how fast does a human get paged?</li>
    <li>What will it never do without asking me first?</li>
    <li>Who maintains it in month six, and what does that cost?</li>
    <li>What is the all-in annual cost including setup, minimums and overage?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Question three is the one most vendors answer badly. A system that can quote a price, promise a timeline or issue a
    credit without a human gate is a liability regardless of how good the voice sounds.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <b>The short version:</b> Avoca is excellent and built for a bigger shop than most people reading this. Below
      $3 million, the real choice is between a cheap app you maintain yourself and a managed service where someone else
      does. Pick based on whether you have a person to own it, not on which demo was more impressive.
    </p>
  </div>

